  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\n*** PATCHY FROST AGAIN TONIGHT? ***\n\nEarly Sunday afternoon, high pressure was sprawled across the Mid-\nAtlantic and Ohio Valley, maintaining tranquil weather over the \nlocal area. Temperatures, in the low 60s at 130pm, will warm another \n1-3 degrees before sinking like a rock tonight as clear skies and \nlight-calm winds make for excellent radiational cooling. Taking this \ntogether with dewpoints in the upper 20s to mid 30s, we suspect low \ntemperatures tomorrow morning - particularly in rural and valley \nlocations - could end up on the low end of guidance. HREF \nprobabilities for temperatures under 37 run 30-60% in valley \nlocations especially near and east of the I-65 corridor, with a few \nsplotches of localized 20+% probabilities for freezing temperatures \nin these areas as well. Given (1) the growing season hasn't \nofficially ended yet and (2) the potential for lows to overachieve \n(be on the low end of the ensemble spectrum), we decided to issue a \nFrost Advisory for most of the area in coordination with neighboring \noffices. In addition, we added \"patchy\" frost to the forecast for \nmuch of the County Warning Area (CWA), and \"areas\" of it in the \nriver valleys.\n\n*** DRY AND MILD THIS WEEK ***\n\nTomorrow will be slightly warmer and drier as forecast soundings \nsuggest we mix a bit deeper, to nearly 850mb (vs ~875-900mb today). \nHighs are slated to approach 70 across most of the area, which is \nnear normal for October 19th. Like today, temperatures will drop \nquickly with the loss of incoming solar radiation (\"insolation\") \nafter sunset, with lows Tuesday morning anywhere from the mid 30s in \nrural, low-lying areas to the low 40s in urban areas.\n\nTuesday and onward, we rinse and repeat as the large-scale weather \npattern remains rather similar with weak ridging across the central \nUS and a cutoff low aimlessly drifting in the western Atlantic. The \none caveat might be a few mid-high clouds Tuesday into Wednesday as \ndeterministic models show a weak shortwave drifting southeast \nthrough the region. Considering this feature will (1) rain out a lot \nof its (Pacific) moisture in the high terrain across the Great Basin \nand (2) dry via downsloping in the High Plains, we're not expecting \nany rain with it, but the clouds may keep us from radiating quite as \nmuch and consequently limit frost potential Tuesday night. In \naddition, they may keep us from mixing quite as deep on Tuesday. \nStill RH values are forecast to drop into the upper 20s to mid 30s \neach day through at least Tuesday, which would raise fire weather \nconcerns if we had any wind; thankfully, winds should generally stay \nless than 10 mph given continued surface high pressure over the area.\n\nThe pattern becomes increasingly amplified late week into the \nweekend, as the central US ridge gets squeezed between a trough over \nthe Northeast and another one lifting out of the Great Basin. This \n\"omega block,\" with the upper ridge centered over the Mississippi \nValley, will keep conditions dry and slightly warmer than normal \nright through the weekend. The only impact we can ascertain during \nthis extended period would be worsening drought conditions due to \nthis continued dry weather. Climatologically, normal would be just \nunder 1 inch of rain per week during mid-late October, so we'd need \nto see at least that much (ideally more) to reverse the ongoing \ndrought, which as of last week's USDM update was classified as D1 \n\"moderate\" across southern Kentucky and D0 \"abnormally dry\" across \nmost of the remainder of the state.",
